From: ysfaculty <haejin.cho@yonsei.ac.kr>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 00:00:32 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 13/13] hbbs, hbbr: log the IDs and relay uuids clients send on
 one line of a bounded length

hbbs logged the ID a client sent as it was: a punch hole request with
the wrong key, which needs neither key nor ticket, logged its ID whole
("Authentication failed ... for peer <id>", up to the 64 KiB message
bound), and a registration, whose ID only has to be 6 to 253 bytes long,
logged it in update_pk, store_pk, IP change and uuid mismatch lines.
hbbr took any relay uuid, up to 128 KiB, kept it as the key of its
waiting list and logged it ("New relay request <uuid>", "... got
paired"). A line break in either starts a log line of the client's own,
which journald keeps as an entry of its own, and which the operator's
checks (grep ERROR, the count of "got paired", the update_pk address
check) read as hbbs's or hbbr's.

- common.rs logged(): a value as it is when it is printable ASCII without
  spaces and at most 64 characters, as every real ID is (the update_pk
  lines the runbook and the E2E grep stay the same), else quoted with
  Rust's escapes and cut at 64 characters. Used in those five lines.
- A punch hole request for an ID over 253 bytes, which no peer can have
  registered, is answered with ID_NOT_EXIST before anything logs it; a
  relay request for one ends its connection, as a bad relay uuid does.
- hbbr refuses a relay uuid outside hbbs's rule (letters, digits, - and
  _, at most 128) with "Drop RequestRelay from <addr>: Invalid relay UUID
  (length N)" (WARN) and closes the connection, before the waiting list
  or the log hold it.

tests/untrusted_input.rs: IDs with a line break in a wrong-key punch
hole request, a registration and a uuid mismatch appear escaped on one
line; over-long IDs in a punch hole or relay request are not logged; a
real ID stays as it is. tests/relay_pairing.rs: a uuid with a line break
and one of 129 bytes are refused and not logged, a client's uuid still
pairs.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
